WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:03.600
Hello there!
Ms. Coffee Bean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.640
the audience has spoken! The last video&nbsp;
was about positional embeddings with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.600
sines and cosines, as it was introduced&nbsp;
in the “Attention is all you need paper”.&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.720
You wrote us in the comments that you want&nbsp;
to know even more about positional encodings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.880
so here it is! Again, a video about them!
This video is about the question:&nbsp;

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:32.400
When to add or when to concatenate positional&nbsp;
embeddings to the initial input representation?&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.640
Then we will talk a bit about&nbsp;
learned positional embeddings.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:42.160
So welcome to this AI Coffee Break, your&nbsp;
coffee infusion enabling you to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.600
a positional embedding expert.
Or not.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:53.200
First, lets’ dive a little bit into the two&nbsp;
ways to handle positional embeddings in general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.880
In the last video, we showed the&nbsp;
“attention is all you need”-way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:03.840
where positional embeddings are added to the&nbsp;
vector initial representations of tokens.&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.920
Then we have seen what an act&nbsp;
of balance this addition is.&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:14.400
By the way, if you did not watch the video, now is&nbsp;
the time, go watch it and come back here for more.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.960
No, Ms. Coffee Bean, not you! You&nbsp;
stay here and do this video with me.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.280
So when adding the positional&nbsp;
encodings to the vector representation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.880
ideally they contain some semantic information&nbsp;
about the token already, but we would like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.000
to make the positional information strong&nbsp;
enough so the model does not overlook it.&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.720
And also not too strong so it does not overtone&nbsp;
the semantic similarities in that vector space.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.080
A proof that this is indeed an act of balance is&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:49.360
the division by 10,000 in the&nbsp;
arguments of the sines and cosines.&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.640
Finding the correct order of magnitude&nbsp;
of the frequency is not straightforward.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:00.480
And all these problems come why? Because we&nbsp;
add positional embeddings to word vectors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.200
and risk mixing up information.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.160
So of course, people have been also&nbsp;
trying other things to counter this,&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.560
like concatenating positional&nbsp;
embeddings instead of adding them.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:18.000
This opens a whole new dimension of possibilities,
quite literally.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.440
Now the semantic information has&nbsp;
its own dimensions to live in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.800
and positional information is encoded&nbsp;
somewhere else, in an orthogonal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.720
thus unrelated space to the semantic one.
This completely avoids an information mess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.080
but comes at a cost: memory, parameters,&nbsp;
runtime, all the bad things!!&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.240
We could let the positional embeddings&nbsp;
have their own dimensions in two ways:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:50.240
If we have the parameter budget, we could&nbsp;
add extra dimensions for encoding position.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.760
In terms of compute, it is&nbsp;
like going from BERT base&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.040
to BERT large.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.440
This luxury comes at a cost, where these&nbsp;
extra dimensions will then negatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.000
impact the memory and computation&nbsp;
footprint down the line in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.440
attention computation and number of neurons&nbsp;
in the fully connected layers afterwards.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.160
The other option is to take some of the&nbsp;
dimensions away from the word vectors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.920
and use them only for positional information; this&nbsp;
means that the semantic representational capacity&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.640
of the model is reduced; it's like&nbsp;
going from BERT large to BERT base.&nbsp;

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:30.080
So to take a bird-eye view again: adding&nbsp;
positional embeddings to word vectors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:36.240
is for the poor but balanced ones that&nbsp;
do not afford the extra model capacity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:42.000
but can keep their position on the thin line&nbsp;
between semantic or positional takeover.&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.400
Concatenation is for the rich, in the sense&nbsp;
that they have larger GPUs with more memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.960
or perhaps are lucky with simpler&nbsp;
tasks that do not need a model with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:59.360
greater expressivity to better capture&nbsp;
[mhm] memorize semantic information.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:04.160
Maybe adding and concatenating are not&nbsp;
that different after all. At least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:10.480
this is perhaps why there is no clear winner&nbsp;
here when we think about performance on tasks of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.840
added versus concatenated positional embeddings.&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.280
What if in these high dimensions that we&nbsp;
cannot imagine, the word vectors and positional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:25.280
embeddings are almost orthogonal anyway?
So it would be actually safe to add them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:29.760
because the model could disentangle&nbsp;
them in the next layers already.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.600
Ms. Coffee Bean isn’t sure about this though.
But one thing is clear: concatenation ensures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.560
orthogonality and no mix-up. Adding&nbsp;
the positional embeddings does not&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.040
and it still seems to work just fine
with a little bit of tuning&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.400
and has lower computational cost.
But ok, I get it. You do not want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.720
do the tuning yourself. So why shouldn't&nbsp;
you do what every machine learner does?&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:00.880
Let the transformer figure out for itself what&nbsp;
it needs as positional embeddings Let the child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.000
or the machine learn by itself!
And here we are already in the realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:13.040
of learned positional embeddings; a realm visited&nbsp;
by language models like BERT, RoBERTa, GPT-2,&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:18.080
and where image models like ViT&nbsp;
and DeiT have experimented too.&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.040
Especially when you have no idea about how to&nbsp;
hand-craft positional encodings for your data&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:28.400
because you’re working with modalities that&nbsp;
are not so explored in terms of positional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:34.880
embeddings like text is… why not learn them?
Interestingly enough, this idea of learnable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:40.720
positional embeddings precedes the “Attention&nbsp;
is all you need” paper and aims to learn some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:46.960
vectors, which you can add to your input with&nbsp;
the same procedure as with any other parameter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:53.760
in your model: with SGD and backpropagation.
And hereby, the network learns interesting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:06:00.160
sometimes surprising things! For example, masked&nbsp;
language modelling objectives in BERT or RoBERTa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:06.400
seem to enforce less structure on the positional&nbsp;
embeddings than autoregressive models like GPT-2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:12.240
A model that must complete the sequence left to&nbsp;
right, has more incentives to focus on position.&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.360
And we can see this in this cosine similarity&nbsp;
plot of positional embeddings in GPT2;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.000
positional embeddings become similar to&nbsp;
their neighboring position, similarity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.840
which grows weaker while going further in the&nbsp;
sequence. But also long-term relations emerge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:34.560
with embeddings having high cosine similarity to&nbsp;
embeddings farther away, in a periodic pattern.&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:40.960
In BERT, this looks very different with positional&nbsp;
embeddings that have only a very narrow pattern.&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:45.200
This kind of makes sense because&nbsp;
replacing a masked word is much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:50.480
more dependent on the immediate neighbors.
But the thing with learned positional embeddings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:56.080
is: They do not seem to consistently&nbsp;
outperform their handcrafted variants.&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:59.920
The “attention is all you need” paper,&nbsp;
tried out learned positional embeddings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:04.160
long before BERT but it did not seem&nbsp;
to outperform their handcrafted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.640
Fourier inspired sinusoidal embeddings.
And you have no idea how much sense it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.720
makes to Ms. Coffee Bean because&nbsp;
it is very similar to the evolution&nbsp;

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:19.040
of self-attention that has been recently&nbsp;
hand-crafted with the Fourier Transform!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:24.720
It seems like research is going in a circle.
Or rather a spiral because there is progress.

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:31.120
But look, the original transformer paper tried out&nbsp;
handcrafted sinusoidal positional encodings and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.920
learned ones, but could not measure&nbsp;
the superiority of one over the other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:42.000
Then BERT, RoBERTa, GPT-2 and so on all came&nbsp;
and advertised learned positional embeddings.&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:48.080
Learned positional embeddings have also been&nbsp;
tried out in “An image is worth 16x16 pixels”&nbsp;

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:54.720
with ViT but also with no significant gains&nbsp;
over the default encodings that were just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.960
numbering image patches, so again&nbsp;
a loss for the learned version.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:03.440
But let’s zoom out a bit because this is&nbsp;
exactly what happens with attention too!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.240
Learned, quadratically scaling attention&nbsp;
has been haunting us for a while now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.160
but lately, there have been&nbsp;
alternatives, like the FNet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:17.360
completely hard-coding self-attention with&nbsp;
the Fourier Transform (where the idea is super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:21.600
similar to sinusoid positional embeddings).&nbsp;
I don’t know, but Ms. Coffee Bean sees these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:27.840
parallels between the opinion oscillation&nbsp;
in the community between learned or not so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:33.920
learned self-attention replacements and between&nbsp;
hard-coded and learned positional embeddings.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:36.560
So what are your favorites? Are you following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.760
the church of added or the church of&nbsp;
concatenated positional embeddings?

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:47.680
Are you more a fan of learned or of hand-crafted&nbsp;
positional embeddings? Let us know in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:53.360
comments. And as requested by you, the next video&nbsp;
will be about relative positional embeddings.&nbsp;

